exec(open('work/scripts/research_notes.py').read().split("if __name__=='__main__':")[0])
s='III. Orthogonality and arithmetic specialization'
add('N38',s,36,'073–077','The live base hypothesis is broader than the Hua summary','reading question',r'The theorem starts with “$A$ anneau noethérien $J$-adique (séparé et complet)” and does not explicitly assume that $A$ is regular; “$X$ normal” occurs after the reduction to a normalized trait on scan 075. The Hua entry adds regularity to its summary, so it should be treated as a restricted paraphrase, while the descent and reduction steps for the broader manuscript statement still require checking. The cover’s “(partiel!)” concerns the scope of the orthogonality result and must remain visible alongside the final “CQFD.”',['ORTH','S19'],'differs from the regular-base wording of 36-orthogonality-regular-adic-base; adds the exact hypothesis and scope distinction','orthogonal|regular|adic')
add('N39',s,36,'074','Lifting finite torsion into the ambient group','unproved claim',r'The passage “les ${}_{\ell^\nu}G$ sont étales de t.f. sép.” is used to insert the unique finite étale lift of ${}_{\ell^\nu}S$ into the torsion of $G$. A complete proof should specify why the flat finite-type commutative group hypotheses give the required étaleness and why the henselian lifting is an open-and-closed finite part, compatibly in $\nu$; the preceding amendment from “lisses” to “plats” makes this more than a harmless change of wording.',['ORTH'],'adds a missing finite-level lifting justification to the orthogonality lead','orthogonal|finite étale|torsion')
add('N40',s,36,'075–076','The finite-field weight computation is the decisive local step','unpursued implication',r'The comparisons “$q^2\,\mathrm{id}$” against “$q\,\mathrm{id}$”, then “$q^{3/2}$” against “$q$”, isolate the arithmetic reason for vanishing. The reduction to a torus extension of an abelian variety also requires disposal of the unipotent part and passage to a finite field extension; recording these operations explains exactly which features of $S$ the argument uses.',['ORTH','N91'],'adds the mechanism behind the Hua summary; differs from the Greenberg-thickness lead, which supplies no such weight calculation','orthogonal|weight|Greenberg|Néron')
add('N41',s,36,'076','A conditional spectral criterion abstracts the weight argument','own idea',r'For a Frobenius-equivariant bilinear map $V\times W\to L$ over $\mathbb Q_\ell$, it is enough that the spectra of $F$ on $V\otimes W$ and on $L$ be disjoint: an intertwiner is then zero, without a semisimplicity assumption, by coprime annihilating polynomials. Applying this after tensoring the manuscript’s finite free Tate modules with $\mathbb Q_\ell$, and using torsion-freeness of the target, recovers integral vanishing whenever the stated spectral separation holds.',['ORTH'],'adds an abstract proof criterion to the orthogonality lead','orthogonal|Frobenius|weight','high','the intertwiner argument is elementary linear algebra; its geometric application is conditional on the stated spectra and torsion-freeness')
add('N42',s,36,'074–075','Reduction through one-dimensional specializations','unproved claim',r'The sequence “dominant par un anneau de val. discret”, then a point with “$\dim\overline{\{z\}}=1$”, is the geometric bridge to the finite-field lemma. Its proof must verify the existence of the required specialization meeting $Y$, preservation of the lifted Tate submodules under pullback, and sufficient detection of the pairing on maximal points of $U$; the later normalization argument establishes normality of the new trait, not of the original adic base.',['ORTH'],'adds the precise reduction obligations and prevents importing the context’s regularity hypothesis into the frozen text','orthogonal|regular|specialization')
add('N43',s,36,'076–077','A single model for all torsion levels','unproved claim',r'After spreading out the groups and a successive-extension decomposition, the page says “Mais les ${}_{\ell^\nu}S_i$ sont encore finis sur $Y_i$” and concludes that all data descend to one finite-type model. Descent at each fixed $\nu$ does not by itself produce one index working for every $\nu$; the decomposition of $S_i$ is the proposed source of that uniformity and needs an explicit argument.',['ORTH'],'adds a uniformity issue not recorded in 36-orthogonality-regular-adic-base','orthogonal|finite|model')
add('N44',s,36,'077','A route to uniform finiteness','own idea',r'One way to complete the preceding step is to descend a finite filtration of $S$ by toric, abelian, and unipotent pieces together with the relevant exactness data, then prove finiteness of multiplication by $\ell$ on that model. Iteration of this one finite morphism would give finiteness of every $\ell^\nu$-torsion kernel at the same index, avoiding an infinite sequence of enlargements of the model. This is a proposed repair, conditional on establishing the descended filtration and the finiteness assertion for its extensions.',['ORTH'],'adds a possible completion of the all-level descent step','orthogonal|finite|descent','medium','the iteration is valid once multiplication by ell is finite, but the required compatible filtration and finiteness over the model are not proved here')
add('N45',s,36,'081; comparison 074–077','The cancelled strict-henselian draft is not a second proof','withdrawn in the same text',r'The whole theorem and proof attempt on scan 081 are crossed out, including the margin “Pour $i$ grand, ils sont bijectifs.” The later-positioned draft should therefore be compared with the live argument on scans 074–077, whose spreading step is more elaborate, rather than counted as an independent verification of stabilization or orthogonality. The order of the sheets alone does not establish their order of composition.',['ORTH','S19'],'adds the draft/live distinction to the orthogonality record','orthogonal|draft|SGA')
add('N46',s,36,'079','Equivalence of the two corollaries is left to the reader','unproved claim',r'The assertion “les deux corollaires sont à priori équivalents, comme on constate aisément” connects orthogonality for a divisor correspondence on $A\times A\prime$ with the polarized self-pairing case. A proof should describe the passage through a product and a polarization, including denominators or isogenies and the recovery of integral $\ell$-adic vanishing from a rational argument.',['ORTH'],'adds an omitted equivalence argument to the orthogonality lead','orthogonal|polarization|coroll')
add('N47',s,36,'079–080','Finite-level nonvanishing survives limit-level orthogonality','unpursued implication',r'The manuscript expressly says “les accouplements eux-mêmes ne sont pas nuls en général” although their projective-limit pairing vanishes. The dual point $x\prime$ detecting a nonzero torsion point after a finite extension need not belong to a compatible tower of rational division points, so this is not a retraction of the Tate-module theorem. It suggests recording which finite torsion classes lift to such towers before interpreting the vanishing at finite level.',['ORTH'],'adds a limitation absent from the Hua orthogonality summary','orthogonal|torsion|limit')
add('N48',s,36,'080','The field is enlarged but the final displayed label is not','reading question',r'The sentence says “quitte à agrandir $K$ en $K\prime$” but the following pairing retains ${}_{\ell^\nu}A\prime(K)$. Reading that final $K$ as a renamed enlarged field is mathematically natural, but the frozen transcription preserves the written label; a future comparison should distinguish this notation issue from a claim about nonvanishing over the original field.',['ORTH'],'adds a source-level qualification to the finite-level counterexample discussion','orthogonal|torsion|field')
add('N49',s,36,'073–079','Vanishing does not by itself identify an orthogonal complement','own idea',r'The equation “$\varphi_\xi(M^I,M_0^I)=0$” supplies an inclusion in an orthogonal complement. Equality would additionally need rank and integral saturation information, so neither the word “orthogonalité” nor “CQFD.” licenses replacing the stated vanishing by a perfect-duality or exact-complement theorem. This explains why a partial orthogonality theorem can be complete as a proof of its own statement.',['ORTH','H9'],'adds a scope restriction; differs from a lattice-duality or Hodge-index conclusion','orthogonal|H9|duality','high','bilinear vanishing alone entails only inclusion, and equality requires extra information')
s='IV. Vertical divisors and the component-group pairing'
add('N50',s,36,'083','Two different exact-sequence defects','unpursued implication',r'The displayed formulas “$\Phi=\operatorname{Ker}u\simeq\mathbb Z/d\mathbb Z$” and “$\Psi=\operatorname{Coker}u$” separate a cyclic kernel from the finite component quotient in the curve case. This is structurally related to the vertical-divisor group $\Gamma$ of scans 065–071, but identifying its invariant-cycle subgroup $R$ with $\Psi$ would confuse a kernel with a cokernel and is not asserted on the page.',['H1','N91'],'adds a comparison of the curve lattice with the H1 defect; differs from any identification of the two defect groups','invariant-cycle|Picard|Néron|Greenberg')
add('N51',s,36,'083–084','The intersection formula omits u in its first occurrence','reading question',r'Scan 083 writes “$(\beta\circ\alpha)((n_i)_i)$”, while the displayed diagram gives $\alpha:\mathbb Z^r\to E$ and $\beta:E^*\to\mathbb Z^r$, so these arrows are not directly composable. Scan 084 explicitly uses “$\beta u\alpha$”; that suggests the missing map in the earlier formula, but it remains a reading/correction question in these notes and does not alter the frozen transcription.',['H1','H9'],'adds an internal formula discrepancy; the context provides no independent resolution of the ink','intersection|lattice|H9|invariant-cycle')
add('N52',s,36,'083','The Néron hypotheses are deliberately rearranged','withdrawn in the same text',r'The concluding paragraph first deletes the perfect-residue-field or coprime-multiplicity condition from the finiteness claim, then inserts “Si de plus $\kappa(s)$ est parfait ou $(d^t,p)=1$” for the Néron-model assertion. The live structure is local factoriality for the stated finite-group conclusion, with the additional alternative for the Néron conclusion; combining the deleted and inserted conditions into one undifferentiated assumption loses that distinction.',['N91','H1'],'adds exact hypothesis bookkeeping to the Néron/Picard context','Néron|Picard|multiplicit|Greenberg'),
add('N53',s,36,'084','Weighted transpose and symmetry are distinct claims','unpursued implication',r'The pairing “$\sum\delta_i n_i n\prime_i$” makes $\lambda$ and $\mu$ transposes, while the displayed sum “$\sum n_i n\prime_j(C_i,C_j)$” proves self-adjointness of $u$ from symmetry of intersections. The deleted word “parfait” matters: a weighted integral pairing need not be unimodular, even when its rational extension is nondegenerate. This is a concrete lattice calculation related in method to WG H9, but it does not prove that hunch’s higher-codimension Hodge-index statement.',['H9','WG2'],'adds a divisor-level lattice mechanism; differs from the broader unreviewed Hodge-index and projector plans','H9|lattice|Hodge|projector|endomorphism')
add('N54',s,36,'084','An explicit discriminant pairing under the stated perfectness assumptions','own idea',r'When $E$ is free, $E^*=\operatorname{Hom}(E,\mathbb Z)$, and the self-adjoint map $u:E\to E^*$ has finite cokernel, define $b(\bar x,\bar y)=y(u_{\mathbb Q}^{-1}x)\bmod\mathbb Z$ for $x,y\in E^*$. Changing representatives changes this value by an integer, symmetry follows from self-adjointness, and the dual-lattice description proves that the resulting pairing on $\operatorname{Coker}u$ is perfect. This supplies the algebra behind the page’s “autodualité canonique”, conditional on those integral hypotheses; it does not yet identify the form with the geometric biextension obstruction.',['H9','H1'],'adds an explicit construction to the lattice lead without settling the geometric comparison','lattice|H9|duality|invariant-cycle','high','well-definedness and perfectness follow directly from an injective finite-index self-adjoint map between mutually dual free lattices')
add('N55',s,36,'083–084','A rank-one quotient test fixes the discriminant sign','own idea',r'For the algebraic model $d=(1,1)$, $\delta_1=\delta_2=1$, and intersection matrix $\begin{psmallmatrix}-m&m\\m&-m\end{psmallmatrix}$ with $m>0$, take $[e_1]$ as generator of $E=\mathbb Z^2/\mathbb Z(1,1)$ and $(1,-1)$ as the dual generator of $E^*$. Then $u=-m$, $\Psi\simeq\mathbb Z/m\mathbb Z$, and the preceding convention gives $b(\bar a,\bar b)=-ab/m\bmod\mathbb Z$. This is a useful sign and normalization test, without asserting that every such matrix is realized by a family in the manuscript’s hypotheses.',['H9','H1'],'adds a small computable model to the component-lattice comparison','lattice|H9|invariant-cycle','high','the quotient, dual basis, and induced one-by-one matrix can all be checked directly')
add('N56',s,36,'084','Nontrivial residue weights require a different integral comparison','own idea',r'If some $\delta_i\ne1$, the index of the weighted lattice in its dual can contribute an additional finite defect, so the same discriminant formula cannot simply be asserted to give the manuscript’s canonical autoduality on $\Psi$. A useful next calculation is to retain the two lattice inclusions and compare their cokernels by the snake lemma, separating weight-index torsion from the cokernel of $u$.',['H9','H1'],'adds a proposed extension beyond the delta_i=1 case; does not establish the war-game hunch','lattice|H9|defect','medium','non-unimodularity is immediate, but the geometric identification and the exact correction group depend on the specific weighted lattices')
add('N57',s,36,'084','The geometric meaning of the discriminant form is asserted','unproved claim',r'The final sentence says that the canonical autoduality “exprime l’obstruction” to extending the biextension. Proving this requires comparison of the lattice construction with an obstruction defined from the generic Poincaré biextension, including independence of choices and the sign convention; symmetry of the intersection matrix alone does not establish that comparison.',['H9','S19'],'adds the missing bridge between the displayed lattice algebra and its biextension interpretation','biextension|H9|SGA')
add('N58',s,36,'061–071, 083','A published Picard comparison does not resolve every kernel','reading question',r'The request “demander Raynaud si Ker est donc $\mathbb Z/d\mathbb Z$” should remain open as a manuscript question even after comparison with Raynaud’s published Picard results. In particular, the later statement involving $P^\tau$ cannot silently replace a kernel defined with the full Néron–Severi quotient, and similarly named integers $d\prime$ must be matched by definition. The precise short references “th. 3” and “prop. 7(ii)” have not been identified in an original announcement.',['H1','S19'],'adds a bibliographic and definitional restriction to 36-h1-invariant-cycle-defect','Picard|invariant-cycle|Raynaud','',None,['P1'])
add('N59',s,36,'083–084','Later comparison results require their own curve hypotheses','unpursued implication',r'The page’s “autodualité” has a precise later comparison target in the intersection-form treatment of Grothendieck’s pairing on Jacobian component groups. Such a comparison must retain the regular-model and rational-point hypotheses in the cited later theorem, rather than using it to certify all hypotheses or all residue weights of these manuscript sheets.',['H9','N91','S19'],'adds a bounded bibliographic comparison; does not promote H9 or the earlier Néron lead to a proved result','lattice|Néron|H9|SGA',bib=['P4'])
render()
